{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":82190,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"abb6b2c9-8734-4736-bf89-4821205432e3","total_cost_usd":0.8469679999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64669,"cache_read_input_tokens":20672,"output_tokens":5651,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64669,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5651,"cache_read_input_tokens":20672,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64669,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64669},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":48547,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.048657,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5651,"cacheReadInputTokens":20672,"cacheCreationInputTokens":64669,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.798311,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"The config flies: the base ship survives its own launch, both routes reach the band, the module is forced to aerobrake and every shield tier survives one — but the module needs two skims from the top rather than the GDD's one, the capacity window is 2.7% wide rather than 32%, the shipping catalog is roughly 4x too light so the full hold neither doubles ship mass nor loads the canopy, and the exploration grid says an unstaged braking pass is unsurvivable almost everywhere (8% of 5,184 worlds), which is a design finding and not a tuning one.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"module_needs_two_skims_from_the_top\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 tabulates the module at 268.8 on a plunge and 196.5 after 'one skim, then commit', against a maxed capacity of ~235 — a one-pass aerobraking finale.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Flown at commit_floor_m 8000 the module reads plunge/1-skim/2-skim/3-skim as 223.7/208.2/204.4/205.8 (bottom), 268.5/224.4/213.9/222.4 (middle), 335.5/243.2/217.8/238.5 (top). Against the shield ladder 219/221/223 the top-of-band single skim at 243.2 is above every tier; only the two-skim descent at 217.8 survives. The finale is a three-heat-cycle descent from the band top, not two. Note also that peak heat is not monotone in skim count — the third skim is hotter than the second in all three module rows — so 'more passes is always cooler' is false and a player who feathers past two is both hotter and paying the escalating toll.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"capacity_window_is_2_7_percent_not_32\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 asserts a 32% window between the full hold's plunge (203.7) and the module's (268.8), 'bounded by a rule rather than by how finely anyone searched, which is why it is safe to build on'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The window that actually constrains the shield ladder is between the module's best skimmed descent (217.8, top of band) and its cheapest plunge anywhere in the band (223.7, bottom) — 2.7% wide, and the three shield tiers 219/221/223 sit inside it with 1-2 units of clearance each. The full-hold plunge is nowhere near it: 104.3/128.6/163.4, so the 32% figure is measuring the wrong pair. The rule survives — must_skim is true in all three module rows and false in all nine ordinary rows — but it survives on a 5.9-unit margin found by scanning dry mass, not on a structural gap. The economy-balancer's own note records the window closing to 0.8% at dry mass 900 and going negative at 1000.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_pass_unsurvivable_across_the_grid\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 makes the unstaged braking pass a real choice: '3x heat, but the engine is still there and periapsis can be raised or lowered between passes'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Design problem, not a tuning problem. Across 5,184 worlds unstaged_pass_survivable is satisfied in 8.0% — the lowest rate of the nine targets by a factor of two, and every one of the twelve best-scoring configurations fails it (unstaged_peak_heat 247-376 against capacities of the same order as the ship's). On the locked planet it does pass — 71 (bottom) and 78 (middle), both survivable — so the shipped config is on the right side of a line almost nothing else in the space is on. That is worth knowing precisely because it means the mechanic is a property of this planet plus a 3x multiplier, and any future change to unstaged_heat_multiplier, reference_area_m2 or dry mass is far more likely to break it than to be neutral. The top sample was not flown unstaged at all — the array has bottom and middle only — so the hottest case is unmeasured.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_does_not_double_ship_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.6 says the module 'roughly doubles the ship's mass' by contrast with a full hold, and 2.5 designs storage as the difficulty-adding purchase.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The shipping slice averages 30.43 kg per slot, so six slots is 182.6 kg on an 800 kg ship — full_hold_mass_ratio 1.23, not 2.00. Storage tier 2 takes the hold from 6 to 10 slots, adds 121.7 kg, and moves touchdown from 3.22 to 3.42 m/s: both soft on the base canopy, no purchase required, so 'storage raises the profit ceiling and the difficulty of the flight home' is currently only the first half. Fixing it from dry mass is not available — dry_mass_kg 183 gives a staged ballistic coefficient of 37, below the ~50 cliff where first contact erases the orbit and the skim mechanic disappears entirely. The controlling quantity is catalog mass, not ship mass: about 133 kg per slot makes the hold 800 kg on an 800 kg ship, exactly 2.00x, and lands it at 4.11 m/s in the middle of the soft band.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"parachute_has_no_headroom_at_full_hold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3 predicts 4.4 m/s at base storage with a full hold on a 630 m2 canopy at Cd 1.8, and the params claim descent_speed_full_hold_ms 3.23.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The claim now holds against an independent measurement — this is the one place the crew fixed a tautology and it stayed fixed. Area is stated in params, so the sweep flies the physical canopy: measured_full_hold_ms 3.22 against claimed 3.23, delta 0.01 m/s, spread across bands 0.00. But the flown value is 3.22, not the GDD's 4.4, and it is 27% below the soft line rather than near it — the canopy is oversized for the mass under it, which is the same catalog shortfall as above seen from the landing side. The consequence is that the entire Parachute upgrade track exists for one object: the module lands at 6.83 m/s on the base canopy (hard, one degradation state), 5.71 at tier 1 (still hard), 4.84 at tier 2 (soft, pristine). Ordinary hauls land soft at every tier from the start, so two of the twelve purchases buy nothing until the finale.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"one_pass_is_cheapest_everywhere_confirmed\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.4\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 5 withdrew the '2-4 pass optimum' goal as unsatisfiable and 4.4 says the cost question is settled and should not be reopened.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Confirmed, cleanly and at every cell. cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all twelve descent rows, and ablation cost rises monotonically with pass count everywhere: bottom full hold 8.8/15.2/22.1/29.9/38.6 and 253.7 at sixteen passes; top endgame 33.6/53.0/61.9/70.0/81.6 and 261.4 at fifteen. Across the grid skim_benefit_saturates is satisfied in 88.9% and skimming_cools_the_entry in 84.6% — the two highest rates of the nine — so cooling is robust and cheapness is not, exactly as the withdrawal predicted. The design is right to rest the mechanic on feasibility (heat_capacity) rather than cost, and cycle_toll_growth 1.13 reproduces the GDD's 847% feathering figure to within 0.2% (848.7%). Recorded as settled, not as a problem.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"middle_sample_circularises_on_fumes\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.5 wants the arc to be the first launch's route and the first tank upgrade to be what makes orbit a real option; the sweep's target fuel_margin_sane guards the rest.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The bottom sample circularises with 6.9% of tank (109.8 kg) and the middle with 2.9% (46.4 kg) — a 46 kg reserve for a deorbit burn on a 1600 kg tank. The top is honestly arc-only ('out of fuel before circularising', 18.0% margin, 368 s window), which is the progression beat the GDD asks for. But fuel_margin_sane is the third-rarest target in the grid at 18.7%, so thin margins here are a property of the space and not carelessness. The arc figures are healthy and reproduce the design: bottom arc apex 73,749 m, EVA window 125.9 s, margin 29.4% against GDD 2.5's stated 29.5% and 97 s — the window is 30% longer than the document claims, which resolves §4.5's first open question in the generous direction rather than the tight one.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"climb_survives_and_the_starter_capacity_is_now_choosable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.4\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 4.4 risk 3: the starter shield's capacity is unchosen and decides whether the first launch looks alarming or looks like nothing at all; the climb was measured at 47.5 bar units.\",\n      \"measured\": \"climb_peak_heat is 50.0/47.5/46.7 across the three ascents, peaking at 19,459 m and 874 m/s, with climb_survivable true on all three — the middle sample reproduces the GDD's 47.5 exactly. Against heat_capacity 219 that is 21.7% of the bar, not the half-bar the document designed the lesson around. The risk the GDD named has landed on the 'looks like nothing at all' side: the first flight's most alarming moment fills a fifth of the gauge. This cannot be fixed by lowering starter capacity, because the same number has to clear the empty ship's 147.8 plunge from the top and sit above the module's 217.8 skimmed floor — one parameter is carrying the first lesson and the endgame gate simultaneously, and the endgame has the stronger claim. Across the grid launch_survives_itself is satisfied in only 34.0% of worlds, so surviving the climb at all is the scarce property here and the shipped config has it.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"debris_catalog.pieces[].mass_kg\",\n      \"current\": 30.43,\n      \"proposed\": 133,\n      \"reason\": \"Raise the shipping slice's mean mass per slot ~4.4x so a full hold is 800 kg on an 800 kg ship — this is the single change that fixes GDD 2.6's doubling, restores the parachute's headroom (full hold lands 4.11 m/s instead of 3.22), and gives Storage tier 2 a real landing cost; it costs a catalog rewrite and must hold module_mass / (base_slots * slice_mean) near 4.5 or the capacity window closes from below.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"gdd.2.2.module_descent_prose\",\n      \"current\": \"one skim, then commit\",\n      \"proposed\": \"two skims, then commit (three heat cycles) from the top of the band\",\n      \"reason\": \"The flown numbers say the top-of-band single skim peaks at 243.2 against a maxed 223 and only the two-skim descent at 217.8 survives; the document should state the finale it actually has rather than have the shop's ceiling contradict the prose.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"reentry.heat_capacity\",\n      \"current\": 219,\n      \"proposed\": 219,\n      \"reason\": \"Hold it — but record that the three tiers 219/221/223 sit in a 5.9-unit window (217.8 to 223.7) and each tier's clearance is 1-2 units, so any later change to dry_mass_kg, reference_area_m2 or drag_coefficient_shield must re-fly the module rows before it ships; at dry mass 950 the window closes entirely.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"sweep.unstaged_braking\",\n      \"current\": \"bottom, middle\",\n      \"proposed\": \"bottom, middle, top\",\n      \"reason\": \"The unstaged pass is the grid's rarest target at 8.0% and the top sample — the hottest case and the one the module flies — was never flown unstaged; the mechanic is currently claimed on two of three samples.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"upgrades[parachute].tier_1.cost\",\n      \"current\": 350,\n      \"proposed\": 350,\n      \"reason\": \"Hold the price but relabel the track: with the catalog as it stands both parachute tiers buy nothing until the module (ordinary hauls land soft at 3.22-3.42 m/s from run one), so the shop label should say it is the finale's canopy — or the catalog change above makes the track earn its keep on its own.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The simulator is a point mass with a perfect pilot. Every peak-heat figure assumes the descent planner hits its periapsis exactly; a real player aiming an entry by hand will miss, and the module's margin at the top of the band is 5.2 units of bar (217.8 against 223) — well inside plausible piloting error. Treat 'the module comes home at full upgrades' as feasible-in-principle, not as comfortable.\",\n    \"Peak heat is non-monotone in skim count in all three module rows (bottom 208.2/204.4/205.8, middle 224.4/213.9/222.4, top 243.2/217.8/238.5). That is physically plausible — each skim changes the approach velocity and the next periapsis together — but the 3-skim row being hotter than the 2-skim row by 20.7 units at the top is a large enough reversal that I would want it reproduced before anyone builds a rule on it. If it is a scan artefact in the skim-altitude search rather than physics, the two-skim finding above is affected.\",\n    \"The adopted ablation.skim_heat_multiplier [1, 0.405, ...] is the top sample's measured array applied to all three bands. The bottom flies 0.702 and the middle 0.526, so the model overstates skimming's benefit at the bottom by about 1.7x. optimal_skims.bottom reads 0 by a 0.8% margin (12.35 vs 12.45) that flips to a clear 0 on the bottom's own measured multiplier — the reported tie is an artefact of the single-array schema, not a real near-indifference.\",\n    \"The exploration grid's satisfaction rates are computed over 5,184 worlds including physically silly ones (radius 800 m with a 224 m atmosphere appears in four of the twelve top configs). A rate like unstaged_pass_survivable at 8.0% is a strong signal about how narrow the property is, but the denominator is not a sample of worlds anyone would ship, so read it as 'rare', not as a probability.\",\n    \"Nothing here measures EVA. The 125.9 s window at the bottom sample is time above the band floor, not time spent flying out, latching, and hauling back — GDD 4.5's first open question is untouched by this sweep and remains a judgement only a human in the seat can make. Likewise the module's 68.6 s of continuous jetpack thrust at 0.140 m/s2 is a closed-form bound, not a flown tow.\",\n    \"Touchdown speed is reported as terminal velocity under a fully deployed canopy and is identical across all three bands (3.22 m/s at full hold everywhere). The simulator therefore models no late-deploy penalty, so 'lands under the damage threshold even with a late chute' — GDD 3's fourth sweep question — is not actually answered by these numbers.\",\n    \"I did not re-derive the heat scale. heat_index is normalised so the bottom sample's periapsis-0 empty entry reads 100, while the capacity ladder is measured at commit_floor_m 8000; those are two different bases on one bar and comparing a number from one against a number from the other is the easiest mistake available in this data set.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":79719,"ttft_stream_ms":1659,"time_to_request_ms":203,"type":"result","duration_ms":79735,"uuid":"9e67a148-484b-43d0-870c-2608ef016271"}
